import{_ as a,o as r,c as t,ao as o}from"./chunks/framework.CPgL1n0-.js";const c=JSON.parse('{"title":"Other pure-Julia FFTs","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"explanation/pure-julia.md","filePath":"explanation/pure-julia.md","lastUpdated":null}'),l={name:"explanation/pure-julia.md"};function n(s,e,i,h,F,u){return r(),t("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[o('<h1 id="Other-pure-Julia-FFTs" tabindex="-1">Other pure-Julia FFTs <a class="header-anchor" href="#Other-pure-Julia-FFTs" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Other pure-Julia FFTs {#Other-pure-Julia-FFTs}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>FFTL is not the only FFT written in Julia. The packages on this page have goals of their own and are not part of the <a href="/FFTL.jl/previews/PR69/reference/benchmarks#Benchmarks">Benchmarks</a>, which compare FFTL with the full-featured reference libraries that it would replace as a dependency (FFTW, ducc0, vDSP, cuFFT, VkFFT).</p><h2 id="FFTA.jl" tabindex="-1">FFTA.jl <a class="header-anchor" href="#FFTA.jl" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;FFTA.jl {#FFTA.jl}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><a href="https://github.com/JuliaMath/FFTA.jl" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">FFTA.jl</a> (&quot;Fastest Fourier Transform in my Apartment&quot;) is built for genericity. It transforms arrays of any element type that provides a few basic operations, so the same call computes an FFT of <code>Complex{BigFloat}</code> numbers in high precision, of symbolic variables from Symbolics.jl (which shows the formula of a transform), or of a small <code>SVector</code> from StaticArrays.jl. Its code base is small and readable.</p><h2 id="FFTJ.jl" tabindex="-1">FFTJ.jl <a class="header-anchor" href="#FFTJ.jl" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;FFTJ.jl {#FFTJ.jl}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><a href="https://github.com/oscardssmith/FFTJ.jl" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">FFTJ.jl</a> is an experimental FFT built on SIMD.jl and the AbstractFFTs interface. Several of its ideas have been contributed to FFTL, among them the batched resampling of lengths with a smooth part and a large prime factor (see <a href="/FFTL.jl/previews/PR69/explanation/algorithms#Lengths-with-a-smooth-part">Lengths with a smooth part</a>).</p><h2 id="PureFFT.jl-and-PureFFTGPU.jl" tabindex="-1">PureFFT.jl and PureFFTGPU.jl <a class="header-anchor" href="#PureFFT.jl-and-PureFFTGPU.jl" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;PureFFT.jl and PureFFTGPU.jl {#PureFFT.jl-and-PureFFTGPU.jl}&quot;">​</a></h2><p><a href="https://github.com/el-oso/PureFFT.jl" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">PureFFT.jl</a> started as an investigation of where the speed of FFTW and RustFFT comes from and whether pure Julia can reach it, and aims to become a general-purpose, dependency-free FFT library. It transforms complex data of any length, with Rader&#39;s and Bluestein&#39;s algorithms for primes, has real transforms, and offers autotuned plans. Its documentation explains in detail which technique contributes what to the speed.</p><p><a href="https://github.com/el-oso/PureFFTGPU.jl" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">PureFFTGPU.jl</a> is its companion for GPUs with the same interface: kernels written in Julia with KernelAbstractions.jl, with vendor-specific kernels as package extensions, rather than a wrapper around the vendor library.</p>',9)])])}const d=a(l,[["render",n]]);export{c as __pageData,d as default};
